{% comment %}
  Overrides Horizon's final type variables with Mionas design-system tokens from
  assets/design-tokens.css, rendered after theme-styles-variables so these win the cascade.

  Only size, line-height and letter-spacing are bridged -- the only three values Shopify
  settings can't express (size enum lacks 38/28/22, line-heights are ratios, tracking is
  capped at +/-0.03em vs the brand's 0.02em). Family/weight/case stay on the theme's font
  settings so Shopify keeps hosting and preloading the faces.

  Token names are mirrored in scripts/design-tokens-contract.mjs, which fails the sync if any
  stop existing.
{% endcomment %}
{%- comment -%}
  Archivo 500 (medium). The theme's four font settings only load Archivo at 400/600/800 (plus
  font_modify'd bold), but several design-system tokens (links, buttons, the label text-role
  band) need weight 500. With no 500 face available, CSS font matching silently falls back to
  400 -- no error, just wrong-looking text -- so this derives and emits the 500 face from the
  body font directly. Guarded because font_modify returns blank when a weight isn't in
  Shopify's library for that family.

  Preloaded like snippets/fonts.liquid does for the four settings fonts, so this weight doesn't
  flash the fallback under font-display: swap while the others are already warm.

  Kept in this Mionas-owned snippet rather than snippets/fonts.liquid, which is stock Horizon
  and would conflict with upstream merges.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign body_font_medium = settings.type_body_font | font_modify: 'weight', '500' -%}
{%- if body_font_medium -%}
  {{ body_font_medium | font_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', fetchpriority: 'low' }}
  {% style %}
    {{ body_font_medium | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
  {% endstyle %}
{%- endif -%}
{% style %}
  :root {
    /* Font families: repoint the design system's two primitives at the faces Shopify actually
       hosts/preloads (--font-body--family / --font-accent--family) rather than the design
       system's own --font-family-* tokens, which are inert strings with no loaded font behind
       them. Every component token resolves through these two, so new design-system components
       need no change here. Both names are pinned in scripts/design-tokens-contract.mjs. */
    --font-family-archivo: var(--font-body--family);
    --font-family-oswald: var(--font-accent--family);

    /* The h1-h6 ladder below is this file's own construction, not something the design system
       hands over -- its text roles are a band x size grid with no inherent mapping to heading
       levels. label-* is excluded despite holding the two smallest sizes: that band is "form
       labels and UI chrome, not page content" in the design system, and a merchant's h6 is
       page content -- which is why h5/h6 land on 20/18px rather than an unbroken 18/15 sequence. */

    /* h1 -> display-lg. Set directly rather than through Shopify's type_size_h1 setting:
       display-lg is 48px, exactly Horizon's fluid-size cutoff, so going through the setting
       would reintroduce a clamp() with a derived 44px floor instead of a fixed 48px. */
    --font-h1--size: var(--text-role-display-lg-font-size);
    --font-h1--line-height: var(--text-role-display-lg-line-height);
    --font-h1--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-display-lg-letter-spacing);

    /* h2 -> headline-md. Below the fluid cutoff, so fixed. */
    --font-h2--size: var(--text-role-headline-md-font-size);
    --font-h2--line-height: var(--text-role-headline-md-line-height);
    --font-h2--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-headline-md-letter-spacing);

    /* h3 -> headline-sm (24px), a step of its own distinct from h2's 28px. */
    --font-h3--size: var(--text-role-headline-sm-font-size);
    --font-h3--line-height: var(--text-role-headline-sm-line-height);
    --font-h3--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-headline-sm-letter-spacing);

    /* h4 -> title-lg */
    --font-h4--size: var(--text-role-title-lg-font-size);
    --font-h4--line-height: var(--text-role-title-lg-line-height);
    --font-h4--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-title-lg-letter-spacing);

    /* h5 -> title-md */
    --font-h5--size: var(--text-role-title-md-font-size);
    --font-h5--line-height: var(--text-role-title-md-line-height);
    --font-h5--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-title-md-letter-spacing);

    /* h6 -> title-sm */
    --font-h6--size: var(--text-role-title-sm-font-size);
    --font-h6--line-height: var(--text-role-title-sm-line-height);
    --font-h6--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-title-sm-letter-spacing);

    /* paragraph -> body-md, the band's own stated default for everything that isn't the intro
       line under a display. */
    --font-paragraph--size: var(--text-role-body-md-font-size);
    --font-paragraph--line-height: var(--text-role-body-md-line-height);
    --font-paragraph--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-body-md-letter-spacing);

    /* Buttons — Horizon states no height: it derives one from padding-block plus the paragraph
       line-height (base.css:1255). The design system states height directly (48px) with 24px
       inline padding. Derive the padding-block that yields the design system's height at the
       line-height this bridge already set: (48px - 24px) / 2 = 12px. */
    --button-padding-block: calc(
      (var(--button-size-md-height) - var(--text-role-body-md-line-height)) / 2
    );
    --button-padding-inline: var(--button-size-md-padding);
  }

  /* Font smoothing: the design system renders with the platform default (subpixel AA on
     macOS), but Horizon sets -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased on `body` (base.css:677),
     which inherits everywhere including Mionas markup and reads visibly thinner, independent
     of the Archivo-500 fix above.

     Universal selector + !important because four of the five upstream declarations live in
     files this theme merges from Shopify/horizon, so restating their selectors here would need
     re-copying after every merge and miss anything Shopify adds later. The fifth,
     blocks/corporate-quote-form.liquid, is this theme's own file and had its declaration
     removed outright instead. */
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    -webkit-font-smoothing: auto !important;
  }

  /* Horizon gives button labels the paragraph weight (400, base.css:1245); no Shopify setting
     exposes button weight, so this needs a rule. Spends the token, not a hardcoded value, so
     native Horizon buttons and blocks/mionas-button.liquid stay in sync -- this only renders
     correctly because of the Archivo 500 face emitted above.

     Same selector list as base.css:1229-1232; renders after base.css so it wins on source
     order. .button-unstyled is excluded since it strips styling to read as plain text
     (base.css:1361), matching base.css's own :not() idiom at line 1265. */
  .button:not(.button-unstyled),
  .button-secondary,
  .button-custom,
  button.shopify-payment-button__button--unbranded {
    font-weight: var(--button-base-font-weight);
  }

  /* Footer tagline: a merchant `text` block with font "Accent" reads as the design system's
     tagline line. text.liquid/typography-style.liquid always emit
     `--font-family: var(--font-accent--family)` inline plus `custom-typography` for that
     combination, so this targets that signature directly instead of adding a dedicated block
     type. Scoped to `<footer>` so it can't catch accent-font text elsewhere on the page. */
  footer .custom-typography[style*='--font-family: var(--font-accent--family)'] {
    color: var(--footer-tagline-foreground);
  }
{% endstyle %}

{%- comment -%}
  Reskin of Horizon's native product card, rendered from here rather than layout/theme.liquid so
  adding it touched no native Horizon file -- keeps upstream merges clean. This snippet already
  renders after theme-styles-variables and base.css, the cascade position the bridge needs.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% render 'product-card-bridge' %}

{%- comment -%}
  Every .mionas-button rule, rendered from here rather than blocks/mionas-button.liquid so the
  CSS doesn't depend on that block being on the page -- blocks/mionas-add-to-cart.liquid wears
  the same classes without rendering the block.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% render 'mionas-button-styles' %}
